# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
*.patch text eol=lf
*.config text eol=lf
*.env text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# higoros-overlay: every file here is shipped into the firmware byte-for-byte,
# so the default is "do not touch".  Git cannot work this out on its own:
# `* text=auto` gets the PNG icons right (they contain NUL bytes) but a forced
# `text eol=lf` corrupts them, and an ordinary text file with no extension is
# a coin flip.  Only the files that have to be LF on the router are listed.
#
# Order matters — later rules win, so the blanket -text comes first and the
# script lists override it.
higoros-overlay/** -text
higoros-overlay/**/*.sh text eol=lf
higoros-overlay/etc/init.d/** text eol=lf
higoros-overlay/etc/uci-defaults/** text eol=lf
higoros-overlay/etc/fw_env.config text eol=lf
higoros-overlay/usr/bin/to-failsafe text eol=lf

# local-packages: same reasoning as higoros-overlay — these files are installed
# into the firmware and executed by the router's own shell.
#
# Why this needs saying: `* text=auto` alone leaves an extensionless script at
# the mercy of the local checkout.  With core.autocrlf=true (the default on
# Windows) `h5000m-firstboot` came out of the working tree with CRLF, and every
# `do` became `do\r`.  The device's dash reports that as
# `syntax error: unexpected word (expecting "do")`, so the first-boot script
# does NOT die loudly — the shell never parses it, nothing enables the radios,
# and a freshly flashed unit comes up with no WiFi at all and no error in any
# log.  It was caught only because the file was run on a real device.
#
# Everything under local-packages/ is text (no binaries today); if one is ever
# added, switch this to the higoros-overlay shape above — a blanket `-text`
# first, then explicit eol=lf lines for the files that must be LF on the router.
local-packages/** text eol=lf
